WEBVTT

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:11.618
It is Wednesday, the second of October, 1872.

00:00:11.629 --> 00:00:14.976
Twenty-nine minutes past eleven in the morning.

00:00:14.984 --> 00:00:19.853
On a quiet street in Mayfair a French valet is being hired by a man who runs his life

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:23.687
by the clock, and the valet's watch is four minutes slow.

00:00:23.695 --> 00:00:26.042
Keep that watch in mind.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:30.313
By a quarter to nine tonight, that same man will have staked half his fortune that the

00:00:30.313 --> 00:00:33.239
whole world fits into eighty days.

00:00:33.247 --> 00:00:35.134
Eighty days.

00:00:35.142 --> 00:00:38.003
Nineteen hundred and twenty hours.

00:00:38.011 --> 00:00:41.700
A hundred and fifteen thousand two hundred minutes.

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:44.832
Look at the map: only London is lit.

00:00:44.839 --> 00:00:49.687
Over the next eighty days we will light the rest, one port at a time.

00:00:49.695 --> 00:00:51.029
Jules Verne printed this journey in a Paris newspaper that same winter, as if it were happening. The novel is our guidebook; we travel today. First, Savile Row.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:57.085
Here we are: the corner of Burlington Gardens and Savile Row.

00:00:57.095 --> 00:01:03.043
Verne opens the novel on this spot — 'No. 7, Saville Row, Burlington Gardens, the house

00:01:03.043 --> 00:01:06.416
in which Sheridan died in 1814.'

00:01:06.424 --> 00:01:11.061
He spells the street with two Ls, and he is wrong twice more.

00:01:11.069 --> 00:01:19.548
Sheridan the playwright lived at No. 14, not No. 7, and he died there in 1816, not 1814.

00:01:19.556 --> 00:01:26.403
Watch the doors on the left as we walk: No. 14 carries the plaque, put up in 1881.

00:01:26.411 --> 00:01:29.153
There is no plaque for Fogg anywhere.

00:01:29.161 --> 00:01:33.951
So the novel is our guidebook, not our gospel; we will correct it with a smile whenever

00:01:33.951 --> 00:01:35.416
we can.

00:01:35.424 --> 00:01:41.285
Look in the windows now — bolts of cloth, chalk marks, half-made jackets.

00:01:41.293 --> 00:01:45.469
This was already tailoring country in 1872.

00:01:45.477 --> 00:01:51.411
Henry Poole came to the Row in 1846 and is still here at No. 15; his shop cut the Prince

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:56.719
of Wales a short evening jacket, which is why the world calls it a dinner jacket.

00:01:56.727 --> 00:02:03.214
One small detour in time: on the roof of No. 3, on the thirtieth of January 1969, the

00:02:03.214 --> 00:02:06.180
Beatles played their rooftop concert.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:11.469
Fogg's day, by the clock.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.696
He rises at eight.

00:02:13.704 --> 00:02:17.169
Tea and toast at twenty-three minutes past.

00:02:17.177 --> 00:02:22.664
Shaving-water at nine thirty-seven, at thirty degrees — eighty-six Fahrenheit; the last

00:02:22.664 --> 00:02:26.169
valet brought it at eighty-four and was sacked for it.

00:02:26.177 --> 00:02:30.248
Out of the door at half past eleven, home at midnight.

00:02:30.256 --> 00:02:35.730
Into this house, at twenty-nine minutes past eleven, walks Passepartout: a Parisian of

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:41.454
Paris, about thirty, once a wandering singer, a circus-rider who vaulted like Léotard,

00:02:41.454 --> 00:02:46.875
a rope-dancer like Blondin, then a fireman in Paris, then ten English households in five

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:48.367
years.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:56.204
From Savile Row, Fogg walks: five hundred and seventy-five steps with the right foot,

00:02:56.204 --> 00:03:01.046
five hundred and seventy-six with the left — one thousand one hundred and fifty-one, and

00:03:01.046 --> 00:03:03.012
he never hurries.

00:03:03.022 --> 00:03:08.219
We are on Pall Mall now, walking west through clubland, one of the first streets in London

00:03:08.219 --> 00:03:11.751
to be lit by gas, in 1807.

00:03:11.759 --> 00:03:18.338
Watch the left side: the Athenaeum at 107, then the Travellers at 106, then the palazzo

00:03:18.338 --> 00:03:26.396
with the tall windows — the Reform Club, 104 Pall Mall, by Charles Barry, opened 1841.

00:03:26.404 --> 00:03:30.751
Fogg made it 1,151 steps.

00:03:30.759 --> 00:03:33.264
We make it about a kilometre.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:41.518
Here is the front today; here is Barry's elevation; and here, kept small, is the saloon

00:03:41.518 --> 00:03:48.063
as engraved in 1841 — we have no footage of the inside, so the engraving carries it.

00:03:48.074 --> 00:03:49.303
Now Fogg's breakfast, taken here at the club: a broiled fish with Reading sauce, a scarlet slice of roast beef with mushrooms, a rhubarb-and-gooseberry tart, a morsel of Cheshire cheese, and several cups of the tea for which the Reform is famous. Reading sauce was a real thing, from Berkshire — keep this in mind for later. Then he cuts open the Times with a paper-knife and reads.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:56.283
Verne describes the club in loving detail: mosaic floors, a gallery under a dome held

00:03:56.283 --> 00:04:01.796
up by twenty red porphyry columns, blue painted windows, a dining room with nine windows

00:04:01.796 --> 00:04:04.959
onto a garden already gilded with autumn.

00:04:04.969 --> 00:04:07.975
Compare that with Barry's real building.

00:04:07.983 --> 00:04:10.751
Which of these details is real?

00:04:10.759 --> 00:04:14.409
A dining-room with nine windows onto the garden.

00:04:14.417 --> 00:04:16.054
Exact.

00:04:16.062 --> 00:04:21.738
Nine tall casement windows in the coffee-room's south wall, and the garden is still there.

00:04:21.746 --> 00:04:27.685
Half fantasy, half exact — treat the novel that way for the next seventy-nine days.

00:04:27.693 --> 00:04:32.759
And one practical note: you can stand in that saloon yourself, one weekend a year, when

00:04:32.759 --> 00:04:37.377
the club opens for guided tours during Open House London; the caption has this year's

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:38.804
dates.

00:04:38.812 --> 00:04:43.672
The tour, they say, includes one very long staircase.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.147
Evening, at the whist table.

00:04:49.158 --> 00:04:53.618
In the novel — and only in the novel — a gentleman has walked out of the Bank of England

00:04:53.618 --> 00:04:58.250
three days ago with fifty-five thousand pounds, and the Daily Telegraph has printed a

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:02.321
timetable showing the world can be circled in eighty days.

00:05:02.329 --> 00:05:07.874
Andrew Stuart, an engineer, wagers four thousand pounds that it cannot be done.

00:05:07.882 --> 00:05:13.987
Fogg raises him: twenty thousand pounds, on deposit at Baring's — half his fortune — in '

00:05:13.987 --> 00:05:19.289
eighty days or less; in nineteen hundred and twenty hours, or a hundred and fifteen thousand

00:05:19.289 --> 00:05:21.268
two hundred minutes'.

00:05:21.276 --> 00:05:24.913
Look at the card: six names on the memorandum.

00:05:24.921 --> 00:05:31.158
Stuart; the bankers John Sullivan and Samuel Fallentin; Thomas Flanagan, a brewer; Gauthier

00:05:31.158 --> 00:05:35.821
Ralph, a director of the Bank of England; and Phileas Fogg.

00:05:35.829 --> 00:05:41.382
Due back 'in this very room of the Reform Club, on Saturday, the 21st of December, at

00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:46.803
a quarter before nine p.m.' Verne's arithmetic holds: it is exactly eighty days, and it

00:05:46.803 --> 00:05:48.939
really was a Saturday.

00:05:48.947 --> 00:05:53.466
Fogg's motto for the evening: 'The unforeseen does not exist.'

00:05:53.474 --> 00:05:55.900
One footnote from today.

00:05:55.908 --> 00:06:02.492
Baring's, Fogg's bank, was one of London's oldest; it went under in 1995.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:05.150
The wager has outlived the bank.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.101
Look at the three lights.

00:06:10.112 --> 00:06:14.630
The Suez Canal, opened in November 1869.

00:06:14.638 --> 00:06:20.143
The American railroad, joined at Promontory Summit in May 1869.

00:06:20.151 --> 00:06:25.841
The last rail link across India, closed at Jabalpur in March 1870.

00:06:25.848 --> 00:06:31.117
Three doors, opened within ten months, two years before tonight.

00:06:31.125 --> 00:06:33.472
Now watch the line draw.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.643
Add them up: eighty.

00:06:35.651 --> 00:06:39.604
And the map fades back to tonight: only London lit.

00:06:39.612 --> 00:06:44.980
Because Fogg, still at the table, says the sentence that turns the evening. 'The train

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:48.275
leaves for Dover at a quarter before nine.'

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:56.466
Two shirts and three pairs of stockings each, a mackintosh, a travelling-cloak, the red-bound

00:06:56.466 --> 00:07:01.650
Bradshaw's Continental Guide — the world's timetable, more than a thousand pages of trains

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:07.379
and hotels — and a goodly roll of Bank of England notes: twenty thousand pounds.

00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:08.566
Two shirts, three pairs of stockings, and half a fortune.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:16.713
Down to the end of Savile Row and into a cab — a hansom, most likely; Verne never says.

00:07:16.724 --> 00:07:21.934
To the cabby, Fogg is a swell, and this is a fare of about a bob: sixpence a mile, and

00:07:21.934 --> 00:07:25.689
it is two and a half kilometres to Charing Cross.

00:07:25.697 --> 00:07:32.084
Watch the line on the 1872 map, south and east to Trafalgar Square and the Strand.

00:07:32.092 --> 00:07:34.176
Fogg takes a cab.

00:07:34.184 --> 00:07:37.703
They left the club at twenty-five past seven.

00:07:37.711 --> 00:07:41.137
They are at Charing Cross at twenty past eight.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:47.627
The Strand, and ahead the forecourt of Charing Cross.

00:07:47.638 --> 00:07:51.985
Forget olde-worlde London: this was Fogg's newest London.

00:07:51.993 --> 00:07:57.354
The station had opened in January 1864 — eight years old that night.

00:07:57.362 --> 00:08:01.446
The hotel behind the cross, 1865.

00:08:01.454 --> 00:08:04.972
The cross itself, 1865.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:08.077
The Underground, nine years old.

00:08:08.085 --> 00:08:11.380
The Embankment down by the river, two.

00:08:11.388 --> 00:08:13.946
Big Ben, thirteen.

00:08:13.954 --> 00:08:18.235
Almost everything Fogg passes tonight is younger than his valet.

00:08:18.243 --> 00:08:25.112
Look at the roofline: Hawkshaw's single iron span, about 155 metres by 50 — five hundred

00:08:25.112 --> 00:08:31.512
feet long — collapsed in 1905 and was replaced; that is all we will say about it.

00:08:31.519 --> 00:08:34.354
Then the one warm beat of the evening.

00:08:34.362 --> 00:08:38.875
At the station a barefoot woman with a child asks for money, and Fogg hands her the twenty

00:08:38.875 --> 00:08:43.454
guineas he won at whist without breaking stride — four hundred and twenty shillings, or

00:08:43.454 --> 00:08:46.617
about eight hundred and forty cab-miles.

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:55.273
On the left, an engraving from 1872 — the year itself — the hotel and forecourt as Fogg

00:08:55.273 --> 00:08:56.752
saw them.

00:08:56.762 --> 00:08:59.675
On the right, the cross today.

00:08:59.683 --> 00:09:00.952
Six platforms behind it, and still about a train an hour to Dover; you can still buy a ticket for Dover at Fogg's station, though the through route to Paris now leaves from St Pancras.

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:08.014
Twenty past eight, and it is raining — Verne says so: the night is dark and a fine, steady

00:09:08.014 --> 00:09:09.966
rain is falling.

00:09:09.977 --> 00:09:12.206
Who is your new master?

00:09:12.935 --> 00:09:18.619
<v Passepartout>Mr Phileas Fogg, of No. 7 Savile Row, monsieur; I have known him since twenty-nine minutes

00:09:18.619 --> 00:09:20.979
<v Passepartout>past eleven this morning.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:26.371
<v Passepartout>He rises at eight, takes tea at twenty-three past, wants shaving-water at eighty-six degrees

00:09:26.371 --> 00:09:27.811
<v Passepartout>exactly.

00:09:27.819 --> 00:09:32.693
<v Passepartout>I said I had seen people at Madame Tussaud's as lively as my new master. Then he said we were going round the world.

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:40.137
Before the whistle, one question: what is the weather doing tonight?

00:09:40.147 --> 00:09:46.679
Rain, then — Verne's words: 'The night was dark, and a fine, steady rain was falling.'

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:55.366
A postcard from 1905, thirty-three years after Fogg — but the same platforms and the same

00:09:55.366 --> 00:09:58.779
roof: the Paris boat train, waiting.

00:09:58.789 --> 00:10:01.071
Now, tonight.

00:10:01.079 --> 00:10:03.847
Twenty to nine, seated.

00:10:03.855 --> 00:10:09.158
A quarter to nine — the whistle, and we are off, over Hungerford Bridge, the way trains

00:10:09.158 --> 00:10:11.558
still leave Fogg's station.

00:10:11.566 --> 00:10:17.250
Look right through the rain: the Embankment along the river, two years old in 1872, and

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:20.308
beyond it the clock tower, thirteen.

00:10:20.316 --> 00:10:23.624
Brand-new London slipping away in the dark.

00:10:23.632 --> 00:10:29.053
Somewhere out past the suburbs — Verne says Sydenham, though by 1872 the express took

00:10:29.053 --> 00:10:33.505
a shorter line — Passepartout suddenly sits bolt upright.

00:10:33.513 --> 00:10:39.224
He has forgotten to turn off the gas in his room. 'It will burn,' says Fogg, 'at your

00:10:39.224 --> 00:10:40.847
expense.'

00:10:40.855 --> 00:10:42.821
To be continued.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:50.473
Your souvenir of the day: Fogg's last breakfast in London — the meal he ate before betting

00:10:50.473 --> 00:10:52.544
half his fortune.

00:10:52.555 --> 00:10:55.810
Save the card; the recipe is on it.

00:10:55.818 --> 00:11:00.186
Cook it on the evening of the twenty-first of December, and be at table by a quarter to

00:11:00.186 --> 00:11:04.178
nine, because that is when he promised to be back in that room.

00:11:04.186 --> 00:11:09.994
Fogg's other motto, and ours for the road: 'A well-used minimum suffices for everything.'

00:11:10.002 --> 00:11:13.810
Next: Dover, Calais, Paris.
